<h1>Route Parameters in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    Route parameters are dynamic values passed through the URL
    and automatically mapped to action method parameters.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why Route Parameters are Used?</h2>

<p>
    Route parameters are commonly used to send:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>ID values</li>
    <li>Category names</li>
    <li>Usernames</li>
    <li>Product names</li>
</ul>

<p>
    They help create clean and readable URLs.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Basic Route Parameter Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Product → Controller</li>
    <li>Details → Action</li>
    <li>5 → Route Parameter</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Action Method Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
public class ProductController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Details(int id)
    {
        return Content($"Product Id: {id}");
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    MVC automatically binds:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
id = 5
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Route Pattern with Parameters</h2>

<pre>
<code>
{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    The parameter:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
{id?}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    represents an optional route parameter.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Optional Route Parameters</h2>

<p>
    The <strong>?</strong> symbol makes the parameter optional.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
{id?}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This means the URL can work with or without the parameter.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    OR
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/10
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Both URLs are valid because id is optional.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Required Route Parameters</h2>

<p>
    If <strong>?</strong> is removed,
    the parameter becomes mandatory.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Now the URL must contain the id value.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/5
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Valid
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Invalid
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Multiple Route Parameters</h2>

<p>
    MVC also supports multiple parameters.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}/{name?}"
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/5/Iphone
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Action Method Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
public IActionResult Details(int id, string name)
{
    return Content($"Id: {id}, Name: {name}");
}
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Default Parameter Values</h2>

<p>
    Route parameters can also have default values.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
pattern: "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id=1}"
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    If id is not provided:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
id = 1
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    is automatically used.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Route Parameters vs Query Strings</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Route Parameters</th>
        <th>Query Strings</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>/Product/Details/5</td>
        <td>/Product/Details?id=5</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Cleaner URLs</td>
        <td>Less readable URLs</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Preferred for resource IDs</td>
        <td>Preferred for filtering/searching</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>How Route Parameters Work Internally</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Incoming URL
      ↓
Routing Middleware
      ↓
Extract Route Values
      ↓
Bind Values to Action Parameters
      ↓
Execute Action Method
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Parameter Type Conversion</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core automatically converts route values
    to action method parameter types.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
public IActionResult Details(int id)
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
"5" → converted to int
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>What Happens if Type Conversion Fails?</h2>

<p>
    If MVC cannot convert the route value,
    model binding fails.
</p>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product/Details/abc
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    when action expects:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
int id
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This may result in:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Validation errors</li>
    <li>404 responses</li>
    <li>Bad Request responses</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>
        Route parameters are dynamic values passed through the URL.
    </li>

    <li>
        MVC automatically binds route values to action method parameters.
    </li>

    <li>
        {id?} represents an optional parameter.
    </li>

    <li>
        Route parameters create cleaner and SEO-friendly URLs.
    </li>

    <li>
        Query strings are generally used for filtering and searching.
    </li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

<p>
    Route parameters allow ASP.NET Core MVC applications
    to pass dynamic values through URLs and automatically bind them
    to controller action methods.
</p>